/* UI & View Hierarchy — 7 topics */

IPREP.addTopic({
  id: 'vclifecycle', domain: 'uikit',
  title: 'UIViewController Lifecycle',
  summary: 'The callback order, what is safe in each, and the containment API.',
  cards: [
    { d: 'easy', q: 'List the view controller lifecycle callbacks in order.',
      a: "`loadView` → `viewDidLoad` → `viewWillAppear` → `viewWillLayoutSubviews` → `viewDidLayoutSubviews` → `viewDidAppear` → `viewWillDisappear` → `viewDidDisappear`.\n\n`viewWillLayoutSubviews` and `viewDidLayoutSubviews` can each fire **many times**, on every bounds change, rotation, keyboard appearance and constraint update. `viewDidLoad` fires exactly once per view load." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What is safe in `viewDidLoad` and what is not?',
      a: "Safe: one-time setup, adding subviews, installing constraints, wiring targets, starting a data load.\n\n**Not** safe: anything depending on final frames. In `viewDidLoad` the view has been loaded but not laid out, so `view.bounds` is whatever came from the nib or a default, not the real size. Rounding a corner, drawing a gradient, or computing a scroll offset here produces the wrong result on any device the storyboard was not designed for. Do frame-dependent work in `viewDidLayoutSubviews`." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Why is `viewDidLayoutSubviews` tricky, and how do you use it correctly?',
      a: "It fires repeatedly, so it must be **idempotent and cheap**. Setting a `CAGradientLayer` frame there is right. Allocating a new gradient layer there is a leak that adds one layer per keyboard show. Kicking off a network call there is a bug.\n\nThe pattern is: create once in `viewDidLoad`, position in `viewDidLayoutSubviews`, guard anything one-shot with a flag." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'When is `viewWillAppear` called but `viewDidAppear` not?',
      a: "During an **interactive transition that gets cancelled**: swipe back halfway on a navigation controller and release, and the incoming controller gets `viewWillAppear` then `viewWillDisappear`/`viewDidDisappear` without ever appearing. \n\nThis is why analytics screen-view tracking belongs in `viewDidAppear`, not `viewWillAppear`, and why starting an expensive resource in `viewWillAppear` and stopping it in `viewDidDisappear` can leave you with an unbalanced pair." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What is `loadView` for and when do you override it?',
      a: "`loadView` is responsible for creating `self.view`. Override it when building the view hierarchy fully in code and you want a custom root view class:\n\n```\noverride func loadView() { view = ProfileView() }\n```\n\nNever call `super.loadView()` in that override, and never touch `self.view` inside it, because accessing `view` triggers `loadView` and you recurse. If you are using a storyboard or nib, do not override it at all." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'Explain view controller containment and the exact call order.',
      a: "```\naddChild(child)\nview.addSubview(child.view)\nchild.view.frame = ...\nchild.didMove(toParent: self)\n```\n\nAnd to remove:\n\n```\nchild.willMove(toParent: nil)\nchild.view.removeFromSuperview()\nchild.removeFromParent()\n```\n\n`addChild` calls `willMove(toParent:)` for you, which is why you only call `didMove` explicitly on add. Skipping these means the child never receives appearance or rotation callbacks and never gets trait collection updates, which produces the classic 'my child controller's viewWillAppear never fires' bug." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What happened to `didReceiveMemoryWarning` and view unloading?',
      a: "`viewDidUnload` was deprecated in iOS 6; views are no longer purged under memory pressure. `didReceiveMemoryWarning` still fires and is the place to drop caches, decoded images and anything reconstructible. It is not a place to tear down your view hierarchy.\n\nIn modern code, prefer `NSCache`, which evicts automatically, and observe `UIApplication.didReceiveMemoryWarningNotification` where the controller is not the right owner." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'How do trait collections fit into the lifecycle?',
      a: "`traitCollectionDidChange(_:)` (deprecated in iOS 17 in favour of `registerForTraitChanges`) fires when size class, dark mode, dynamic type size or display scale changes. It is the correct hook for anything appearance-dependent that Auto Layout and dynamic colours cannot express.\n\nThe common mistake is doing this work in `viewWillAppear`, which misses in-place changes such as the user flipping to dark mode while your screen is visible." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Where should a view controller start and stop observing?',
      a: "Symmetric pairs, matched to the same lifecycle level:\n- One-time observation with no visual cost: `viewDidLoad` and rely on `deinit`.\n- Expensive or visible-only work such as a location manager, a video player, or a timer: `viewWillAppear` and `viewWillDisappear`.\n\nMixing levels, for example starting in `viewDidLoad` and stopping in `viewDidDisappear`, gives you an unbalanced lifetime and either a leak or a crash on the second appearance." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'What is the difference between `present` and `pushViewController` in terms of lifecycle?',
      a: "A push keeps both controllers in the navigation stack, so the pushed-from controller stays alive and gets `viewWillDisappear`/`viewDidDisappear` but is not deallocated. A modal presentation over full screen does the same to the presenter; a modal with `.overCurrentContext` or a non-fullscreen sheet leaves the presenter **visible**, so it does not receive disappearance callbacks at all.\n\nThat last case breaks code that assumes 'viewDidAppear means I am the visible screen', which is why sheet presentations often produce duplicate analytics events." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { d: 'easy', q: 'Which callback fires exactly once per view load?', choices: ['viewWillAppear', 'viewDidLayoutSubviews', 'viewDidLoad', 'viewWillLayoutSubviews'], correct: 2,
      why: 'The appearance and layout callbacks can each fire many times. viewDidLoad is the one-shot setup point.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Rounding a corner using view.bounds in viewDidLoad gives the wrong radius because:', choices: ['bounds is always zero', 'Layout has not run, so bounds is not the final size', 'Corner radius must be set on the layer', 'viewDidLoad runs on a background thread'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Frame-dependent work belongs in viewDidLayoutSubviews, which runs after layout has resolved the real size.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'Analytics screen views belong in viewDidAppear rather than viewWillAppear because:', choices: ['viewWillAppear is slower', 'A cancelled interactive back swipe fires viewWillAppear without the screen ever appearing', 'viewWillAppear runs off the main thread', 'viewDidAppear fires more often'], correct: 1,
      why: 'The cancelled-transition case produces a will/did-disappear pair with no appearance, inflating your screen-view counts.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Correct containment removal order:', choices: ['removeFromParent, removeFromSuperview, willMove(toParent: nil)', 'willMove(toParent: nil), removeFromSuperview, removeFromParent', 'removeFromSuperview, didMove(toParent: nil), removeFromParent', 'removeFromParent only'], correct: 1,
      why: 'willMove first, then detach the view, then removeFromParent, which calls didMove(toParent: nil) for you.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'A sheet is presented over your controller. Your controller receives:', choices: ['viewWillDisappear and viewDidDisappear', 'No disappearance callbacks, because it stays visible', 'viewDidLoad again', 'Only viewWillDisappear'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Non-fullscreen presentations leave the presenter on screen, so appearance callbacks do not fire. Code assuming otherwise double-counts.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Overriding loadView, you must NOT:', choices: ['Assign to self.view', 'Access self.view before assigning it', 'Create a custom root view class', 'Set a background colour'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Reading self.view triggers loadView, so touching it inside the override recurses infinitely.' }
  ]
});

IPREP.addTopic({
  id: 'viewlayout', domain: 'uikit',
  title: 'UIView Hierarchy & the Layout Pass',
  summary: 'setNeedsLayout, layoutSubviews, display vs layout, and how UIKit batches work.',
  cards: [
    { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the relationship between UIView and CALayer?',
      a: "Every `UIView` is **backed by** a `CALayer` that owns the actual drawing: the backing store, the transform, the corner radius, the shadow. The view adds event handling, the responder chain, Auto Layout participation and an animation-friendly API on top.\n\nSo a view is a thin, interactive wrapper around a layer. Changing `view.layer.transform` directly bypasses UIView animation semantics, which is why the two APIs sometimes disagree." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Explain `setNeedsLayout`, `layoutIfNeeded` and `layoutSubviews`.',
      a: "- `setNeedsLayout()` marks the view dirty and returns immediately. Layout happens later in the current run loop's layout pass. **Cheap, and what you should call.**\n- `layoutIfNeeded()` forces the pending layout to run **synchronously, now**. Use it when you need resolved frames before starting an animation.\n- `layoutSubviews()` is the callback where layout actually happens. You override it, you never call it.\n\nCalling `layoutSubviews` directly is always wrong; calling `layoutIfNeeded` in a loop is a performance bug." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between the layout pass and the display pass?',
      a: "**Layout** resolves geometry: `layoutSubviews` runs top-down, setting frames. **Display** produces pixels: `draw(_:)` runs for views whose contents are marked dirty, rendering into a backing store.\n\nThey have separate invalidation calls: `setNeedsLayout` / `layoutIfNeeded` for geometry, `setNeedsDisplay` / `display` for content. Both flush before the frame is committed to the render server. Confusing the two is why people call `setNeedsDisplay` expecting a reposition and get nothing." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'Describe the full frame lifecycle from a touch to pixels on screen.',
      a: "1. Touch is delivered, your handler mutates state and calls `setNeedsLayout`.\n2. The run loop reaches the **layout pass**: constraints are solved, `layoutSubviews` runs top-down.\n3. The **display pass**: dirty views run `draw(_:)` into their backing store.\n4. **Commit**: the layer tree is encoded and sent over IPC to the render server (`backboardd`).\n5. The render server composites the layers on the GPU and hands the result to the display for the next vsync.\n\nMissing the 16.7ms budget (or 8.3ms at 120Hz) at any stage drops a frame. That is why heavy work in `layoutSubviews` or `draw(_:)` shows up as jank." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between `frame` and `bounds`?',
      a: "`frame` is the view's rectangle in its **superview's** coordinate space. `bounds` is its rectangle in its **own** coordinate space, with origin usually `.zero`.\n\nTwo consequences: setting `bounds.origin` scrolls the content (that is exactly how `UIScrollView` works), and `frame` is **undefined when a non-identity transform is applied**, because a rotated rectangle has no meaningful axis-aligned frame. With a transform, set `bounds` and `center`, never `frame`." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'Why is `draw(_:)` expensive and how do you avoid it?',
      a: "Overriding `draw(_:)` allocates a **backing store** the size of the view in pixels, and forces CPU rasterisation via Core Graphics on the main thread. A full-screen view at 3x is roughly 1170x2532x4 bytes, about 12MB, redrawn every time you invalidate.\n\nAvoid it by composing with layers instead: `CAShapeLayer` for paths, `CAGradientLayer` for gradients, `layer.cornerRadius` for rounding, a `UIImageView` for images. These are GPU-composited with no backing store. Reach for `draw(_:)` only for genuinely custom rendering." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What does `clipsToBounds` cost, and what about `masksToBounds`?',
      a: "They are the same thing: `clipsToBounds` on the view sets `masksToBounds` on the layer. It forces the compositor to apply a clip, which for a simple rectangular bounds is cheap.\n\nIt becomes expensive when combined with a corner radius plus a shadow, because a shadow is drawn outside the bounds and gets clipped away, so people add `shouldRasterize` or a second layer to compensate. The correct pattern is a container view for the shadow and an inner view for the clip." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'What is offscreen rendering and which properties trigger it?',
      a: "Offscreen rendering means the GPU must render a layer into a separate buffer before compositing it, which costs a context switch per frame. Triggers:\n- `layer.masksToBounds` **with** `cornerRadius` on a layer that has sublayers or content\n- `layer.shadowPath` **not** set while a shadow is enabled, so the shape must be derived from the alpha channel\n- `layer.shouldRasterize`\n- Group opacity, masks, and some blend modes\n\nThe cheapest fix is almost always to set an explicit `shadowPath`. Enable Color Offscreen-Rendered Yellow in the simulator's Debug menu to find them." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'When does `layoutSubviews` get called?',
      a: "- The view's own bounds change (not the frame origin alone)\n- A subview is added or removed\n- `setNeedsLayout` or `setNeedsUpdateConstraints` was called and the pass runs\n- The device rotates or the size class changes\n- A scroll view scrolls, which is why `layoutSubviews` on a scroll view is a hot path\n\nIt does **not** fire merely because you moved the view without resizing it. Assuming it does is a common source of stale layout." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'Why does adding a subview inside `layoutSubviews` cause problems?',
      a: "Adding a subview invalidates layout, which schedules another layout pass, which calls `layoutSubviews` again, which adds another subview. You get either an infinite layout loop or a steadily growing hierarchy that manifests as a slow leak while scrolling.\n\nCreate subviews once at init or `viewDidLoad`, and only set their frames in `layoutSubviews`. If you must add conditionally, guard with a flag and understand that you are re-entering the pass." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between `sizeToFit`, `sizeThatFits` and `intrinsicContentSize`?',
      a: "- `sizeThatFits(_:)` is a **query**: given this bounding size, what size would you like? It does not mutate anything.\n- `sizeToFit()` calls `sizeThatFits` and applies the result to the frame. Manual-layout API.\n- `intrinsicContentSize` is the Auto Layout equivalent: the size a view wants based on its content, used to synthesise constraints.\n\nIn an Auto Layout world you override `intrinsicContentSize` and call `invalidateIntrinsicContentSize()` when the content changes." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { d: 'easy', q: 'Which do you call to request layout without forcing it immediately?', choices: ['layoutSubviews()', 'setNeedsLayout()', 'layoutIfNeeded()', 'setNeedsDisplay()'], correct: 1,
      why: 'setNeedsLayout marks dirty and defers to the run loop layout pass. layoutIfNeeded forces it synchronously.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'A view has a rotation transform applied. To reposition it you should set:', choices: ['frame', 'bounds and center', 'frame.origin only', 'anchorPoint'], correct: 1,
      why: 'frame is undefined under a non-identity transform, because a rotated rectangle has no meaningful axis-aligned frame.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'The cheapest fix for a shadow that triggers offscreen rendering is:', choices: ['Enable shouldRasterize', 'Set an explicit shadowPath', 'Reduce shadowOpacity', 'Set masksToBounds to true'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Without shadowPath the GPU derives the shape from the alpha channel offscreen. An explicit path removes the pass entirely.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Overriding draw(_:) on a full-screen view costs you:', choices: ['Nothing, it is GPU accelerated', 'A CPU-rasterised backing store of roughly width x height x scale^2 x 4 bytes', 'An extra view controller', 'Auto Layout invalidation'], correct: 1,
      why: 'That is why layer composition with CAShapeLayer or CAGradientLayer beats custom drawing for common effects.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'Adding a subview inside layoutSubviews typically causes:', choices: ['A compile error', 'A layout loop or a steadily growing hierarchy', 'Nothing unusual', 'An immediate crash'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Adding a subview invalidates layout, which re-enters layoutSubviews, which adds another subview.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'layoutSubviews is NOT triggered by:', choices: ['A bounds change', 'Adding a subview', 'Moving the view without resizing it', 'Device rotation'], correct: 2,
      why: 'Only bounds changes count, not a pure origin move. Assuming otherwise leaves stale internal layout.' }
  ]
});

IPREP.addTopic({
  id: 'autolayout', domain: 'uikit',
  title: 'Auto Layout & Constraints',
  summary: 'The constraint solver, priorities, hugging and compression, and debugging conflicts.',
  cards: [
    { d: 'easy', q: 'What is Auto Layout actually doing?',
      a: "It converts your constraints into a system of **linear equations and inequalities** and solves them with the Cassowary algorithm to produce frames. Each constraint is of the form `item1.attr1 = multiplier * item2.attr2 + constant`, with a priority. The solver finds an assignment satisfying all required (priority 1000) constraints while getting as close as possible to the optional ones." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Explain content hugging versus compression resistance.',
      a: "Both are priorities on a view's intrinsic content size.\n- **Content hugging**: how hard the view resists **growing** larger than its intrinsic size. High hugging means 'stay small'.\n- **Compression resistance**: how hard it resists **shrinking** below intrinsic size. High resistance means 'do not truncate me'.\n\nThe classic use: two labels side by side, one truncating. Raise the compression resistance of the one that must stay whole, or lower the hugging of the one that should absorb the extra space." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What does a constraint priority of 1000 versus 999 change?',
      a: "1000 is **required**: the solver must satisfy it or it logs a conflict and breaks one. 999 and below are **optional**: the solver treats them as goals and minimises the error.\n\nThe practical trick is to make a constraint you might need to break 999, so that when it conflicts the layout degrades gracefully instead of dumping an unsatisfiable-constraints wall of text. It is also how you build 'prefer this height, but allow less'." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'How do you actually debug an unsatisfiable constraints log?',
      a: "1. Read the list: UIKit prints the conflicting set and says which one it broke.\n2. Give your constraints identifiers (`c.identifier = \"card.top\"`) so the log is readable instead of anonymous `NSLayoutConstraint` addresses.\n3. Use the **view debugger**: pause, select the view, and Xcode shows constraints, ambiguity and the runtime frame.\n4. Call `view.hasAmbiguousLayout` and `view.exerciseAmbiguityInLayout()` in the debugger to see which axis is unconstrained.\n\nThe root cause is nearly always a duplicate constraint added twice, or a fixed size fighting an intrinsic size." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between `translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints` true and false?',
      a: "When true (the default for views created in code), UIKit generates constraints from the view's `frame` and `autoresizingMask`. Those generated constraints are required, so they conflict with everything you add.\n\nSet it to `false` on every view you lay out with constraints. Forgetting it is the single most common cause of an unsatisfiable-constraints crash log in a code-based layout. Views from a storyboard already have it false." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'What is the performance profile of Auto Layout, and when does it become a problem?',
      a: "Solving is roughly superlinear in the number of constraints in a connected system, and the solver re-runs whenever anything changes. A single screen with a hundred constraints is fine. A `UITableViewCell` with sixty constraints, re-solved for every cell on every scroll frame, is not.\n\nMitigations: fewer, simpler constraints; avoid deep nesting; use `UIStackView` judiciously (it generates constraints too); prefer fixed row heights where possible; and in the extreme case, lay the cell out manually in `layoutSubviews`, which is what high-performance feed apps actually do." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you animate a constraint change correctly?',
      a: "```\nheightConstraint.constant = 200\nUIView.animate(withDuration: 0.3) {\n    self.view.layoutIfNeeded()\n}\n```\n\nThe key is that you change the constant **outside** the animation block and call `layoutIfNeeded` **inside** it. Animating the constraint mutation itself does nothing, because constraints are not animatable properties; the frames they produce are. Call `layoutIfNeeded()` once before the block too if there is a pending layout you do not want folded into the animation." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'What is a layout guide and why prefer it to a dummy view?',
      a: "`UILayoutGuide` is a rectangle that participates in Auto Layout without being a view: no backing layer, no drawing, no touch handling, no traversal cost. Use it for spacing and alignment where you previously would have added an invisible spacer view.\n\n`safeAreaLayoutGuide`, `readableContentGuide` and `keyboardLayoutGuide` are system-provided ones. Constraining to `safeAreaLayoutGuide` rather than the view edges is what keeps content clear of the notch, the home indicator and the navigation bar." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What does `UIStackView` do for you and what does it cost?',
      a: "It generates and maintains the constraints for a linear arrangement, handling `axis`, `distribution`, `alignment` and `spacing`, and it responds to `isHidden` on arranged subviews by collapsing them, which is genuinely useful.\n\nThe cost is that it is constraints underneath, so a deeply nested stack view hierarchy can be slower than a flat set of constraints, and debugging a conflict inside one is harder because you did not write the constraints. Two or three levels is fine; six is a smell." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'Explain `updateConstraints` and why you usually should not override it.',
      a: "`updateConstraints()` is a batching hook: mark dirty with `setNeedsUpdateConstraints()` and UIKit calls it once before layout. It exists for the case where recomputing constraints is expensive and happens often.\n\nApple's guidance is that changing constraints directly in place is usually faster, because `updateConstraints` adds a whole extra pass. Override it only when you are provably rebuilding many constraints repeatedly. If you do override it, always call `super` **last**." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'How do self-sizing table view cells work?',
      a: "Set `tableView.rowHeight = UITableView.automaticDimension` and provide an `estimatedRowHeight`. The cell's `contentView` must have an **unbroken chain of constraints from top to bottom**, so the solver can derive a height. One missing vertical constraint and the cell collapses or the height is wrong.\n\nThe estimate matters for scroll performance: a bad estimate causes the scroll indicator to jump as real heights replace estimates. Get it close, or supply per-row estimates with `estimatedHeightForRowAt`." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Two labels side by side, the wrong one truncates. You should:', choices: ['Lower the hugging priority of the label that must stay whole', 'Raise the compression resistance of the label that must stay whole', 'Set both to required priority', 'Give both a fixed width'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Compression resistance controls resistance to shrinking below intrinsic size, which is what truncation is.' },
    { d: 'easy', q: 'For a view created in code and laid out with constraints you must set:', choices: ['translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = true', 'translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false', 'autoresizingMask = .flexibleWidth', 'contentMode = .redraw'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Otherwise UIKit generates required constraints from the frame that conflict with everything you add.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'To animate a constraint change you:', choices: ['Change the constant inside the animation block', 'Change the constant outside, then call layoutIfNeeded inside the block', 'Animate the constraint object directly', 'Use CABasicAnimation on the constraint'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Constraints are not animatable. The animation interpolates the frames that layoutIfNeeded produces.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'A UITableViewCell with 60 constraints scrolls badly. The most direct explanation is:', choices: ['Too many subviews', 'The solver re-runs per cell per frame and cost grows superlinearly with constraint count', 'Auto Layout runs on a background thread', 'Cells are not being reused'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Constraint solving is the dominant cost. Flattening the hierarchy, or hand-laying out in layoutSubviews, is the standard fix for feed-scale cells.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'UILayoutGuide is preferable to a spacer view because:', choices: ['It is easier to see in the debugger', 'It has no backing layer, drawing, or touch handling', 'It supports animation', 'It can hold subviews'], correct: 1,
      why: 'It participates in layout with none of the per-view rendering and traversal cost.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'A self-sizing cell collapses to zero height. Most likely cause:', choices: ['estimatedRowHeight is too small', 'The contentView lacks an unbroken top-to-bottom constraint chain', 'rowHeight was set to a fixed value', 'The cell is not registered'], correct: 1,
      why: 'The solver needs a continuous vertical path through the content view to derive a height. One missing link and there is nothing to solve.' }
  ]
});

IPREP.addTopic({
  id: 'cellreuse', domain: 'uikit',
  title: 'UITableView, UICollectionView & Cell Reuse',
  summary: 'The reuse pool, prefetching, diffable data sources, and the bugs reuse causes.',
  cards: [
    { d: 'easy', q: 'Why does cell reuse exist and how does it work?',
      a: "Allocating a view per row would be O(rows) memory and would stutter on every new row. Instead the table keeps a small pool sized to what fits on screen plus a couple. When a cell scrolls off, it goes into the pool keyed by its reuse identifier; when a new row needs a cell, `dequeueReusableCell(withIdentifier:)` returns a pooled one and calls `prepareForReuse()` on it.\n\nSo you have roughly a dozen cell objects serving ten thousand rows." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What must `prepareForReuse` do, and what is the most common reuse bug?',
      a: "Reset any state that will not be unconditionally overwritten in `cellForRowAt`: cancel an in-flight image load, clear the image, reset an accessory, stop an animation, reset a selection highlight.\n\nThe classic bug is the **wrong image appearing on the wrong row**: you start an async image load for row 3, the cell is reused for row 40 before the load finishes, and the completion sets row 3's image on the row 40 cell. Fix it by cancelling in `prepareForReuse`, or by checking the cell's current index path or a token before assigning." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'Why does checking `tableView.indexPath(for: cell)` in the image completion fix the wrong-image bug?',
      a: "It asks the table view what index path that cell object is **currently** displaying, not what it was when you started. If it no longer matches the index path you loaded for, you discard the result.\n\nThe cleaner alternative is a per-cell token: store a `UUID` on the cell when you start the load and capture it in the closure; if `cell.token != captured` on completion, drop it. That is O(1), does not depend on the table, and works for collection views and nested reuse too." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between registering a class and a nib for reuse?',
      a: "`register(_:forCellReuseIdentifier:)` with a class calls `init(style:reuseIdentifier:)`; with a nib it instantiates from the nib each time a new cell is needed. Nib instantiation is measurably slower than a code init because it parses and unarchives.\n\nEither way, once registered, `dequeueReusableCell(withIdentifier:for:)` **never returns nil**, which is why the modern `for indexPath:` variant is non-optional. The old nil-returning variant existed before registration was required." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'What is prefetching and when does it help?',
      a: "`UITableViewDataSourcePrefetching` gives you `prefetchRowsAt` a few screens ahead of scroll, and `cancelPrefetchingForRowsAt` when the user reverses. It is the hook for starting image downloads and expensive decodes before the row is needed.\n\nIt helps when the per-row work is **asynchronous and cancellable**. It does not help if the work must happen on the main thread anyway, and it actively hurts if you start unbounded work and never cancel, because a fast flick queues hundreds of requests." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What does a diffable data source change about your code?',
      a: "You stop implementing `numberOfRows` and `cellForRow`-driven mutation, and instead apply a `NSDiffableDataSourceSnapshot` of section and item **identifiers**. UIKit computes the diff and performs the correct insert, delete, move and reload animations.\n\nThe big win is the elimination of 'invalid number of rows' exceptions, which came from the data array and the batch updates disagreeing. The constraint is that item identifiers must be `Hashable` and **stable and unique**; using a value that changes when the item changes causes the item to be deleted and reinserted instead of reloaded." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'Why must diffable data source identifiers be identity, not content?',
      a: "The diff is computed on identifiers. If your identifier hashes the whole model, then editing a title produces a different identifier, so the framework sees a delete plus an insert rather than an update. You lose the reload animation, lose selection, and lose scroll position stability.\n\nUse a stable `id`. To reflect content changes, call `snapshot.reconfigureItems([id])` (iOS 15+), which re-runs the cell provider without recreating the cell." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What causes the classic \"invalid number of rows in section\" crash?',
      a: "The model and the batch update disagree. You called `insertRows(at:)` for one row but the data source now returns a count that is not old count plus one. It also happens when you mutate the model on a background thread while the table reads it on the main thread.\n\nRules: mutate the model and perform the table update in the **same** main-thread turn, use `performBatchUpdates` so the framework validates the whole set at once, or move to a diffable data source, which removes the class of bug." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'How do you make a complex feed cell scroll at 120Hz?',
      a: "- Flatten the hierarchy; every view costs traversal and composition.\n- Avoid `draw(_:)`; use layers.\n- Precompute heights and text layout off the main thread, cache them per item.\n- Decode images off the main thread at the **display size**, not full resolution; `UIImage(data:)` decodes lazily on first draw, which is on the main thread, so force decode in the background.\n- Avoid offscreen rendering: set `shadowPath`, avoid `cornerRadius` plus `masksToBounds` on complex content.\n- Reuse aggressively; do not build subviews in `cellForRow`.\n\nThen measure with Instruments' Animation Hitches template rather than guessing." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between `reloadData` and `reloadRows`?',
      a: "`reloadData` throws away all cells and starts over: no animation, loses selection and editing state, and re-queries everything. `reloadRows(at:with:)` animates specific rows and preserves the rest.\n\n`reloadData` is the sledgehammer that hides model bugs. If you find yourself calling it after every small change, you are paying a full layout pass to avoid thinking about the diff, and it will visibly flicker on a long list." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'What is a compositional layout and why did it replace flow layout for complex screens?',
      a: "`UICollectionViewCompositionalLayout` describes a layout declaratively as items inside groups inside sections, each with its own size, spacing, orthogonal scrolling direction and supplementary views. A screen with a horizontally scrolling carousel above a grid above a list is a few dozen lines, where a `UICollectionViewFlowLayout` subclass would be hundreds of lines of `layoutAttributesForElements(in:)`.\n\nIt also computes only what is visible, so it scales to large sections better than a hand-rolled layout that precomputes everything." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { d: 'medium', q: 'The wrong image shows on a row after fast scrolling. The root cause is:', choices: ['The image cache is too small', 'An async completion sets an image on a cell that has since been reused for another row', 'prepareForReuse is called too often', 'The table needs reloadData'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Cancel in prepareForReuse, or verify the cell still represents the index path (or match a per-cell token) before assigning.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: '`dequeueReusableCell(withIdentifier:for:)` returns non-optional because:', choices: ['It creates a cell if the pool is empty, and registration is required', 'It crashes on nil', 'Cells are never deallocated', 'It uses force unwrapping internally'], correct: 0,
      why: 'Registration guarantees the table can always construct one, so the API can be non-optional.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'Your diffable item identifier hashes the whole model. Editing a title causes:', choices: ['A smooth reload of that row', 'A delete plus insert, losing selection and animation', 'A crash', 'Nothing, the diff ignores it'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Identifiers must be identity. Use a stable id and reconfigureItems to reflect content changes.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: '"Invalid number of rows in section" means:', choices: ['The cell height is wrong', 'The data source count and the batch update do not agree', 'You forgot to register a cell', 'The table view is nil'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Mutate model and table in the same main-thread turn, or use a diffable data source, which removes the failure mode.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'UIImage(data:) decoding is a scroll hazard because:', choices: ['It always fails for large images', 'Decoding is lazy and happens on the main thread at first draw', 'It blocks the network', 'It caches too aggressively'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Force the decode on a background queue at display size, so the main thread only composites an already-decoded bitmap.' },
    { d: 'easy', q: 'Prefetching actively hurts when:', choices: ['The list is short', 'You start unbounded work and never cancel on reversal', 'Cells are self-sizing', 'You use a diffable data source'], correct: 1,
      why: 'A fast flick queues hundreds of requests. cancelPrefetchingForRowsAt exists precisely to bound this.' }
  ]
});

IPREP.addTopic({
  id: 'responder', domain: 'uikit',
  title: 'Responder Chain, Hit Testing & Gestures',
  summary: 'How a touch finds its view, how events bubble, and why taps sometimes do nothing.',
  cards: [
    { d: 'easy', q: 'What is the responder chain?',
      a: "An ordered list of objects that get a chance to handle an event. It typically runs: first responder → its superviews up the hierarchy → the view controller → the window → the application → the app delegate. Each `UIResponder` either handles the event or calls `super`, which passes it along.\n\nIt is what makes `sendAction(_:to:nil:for:)` with a nil target work: the action travels the chain until something responds to that selector." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Describe hit testing, precisely.',
      a: "UIKit calls `hitTest(_:with:)` on the window, which recurses **depth-first in reverse subview order** (topmost first). For each view it first calls `point(inside:with:)`; if false, that whole branch is skipped. The deepest view returning itself wins and becomes the touch target.\n\nA view is skipped entirely if `isHidden`, `alpha <= 0.01`, or `isUserInteractionEnabled == false`. Note this is separate from the responder chain: hit testing **finds** the target, the responder chain **propagates** from it." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'A button outside its parent view bounds does not respond. Why, and how do you fix it?',
      a: "Because `point(inside:with:)` on the parent returns false for that location, so hit testing never descends into the button, whether or not `clipsToBounds` is set. Visibility and touchability are different systems.\n\nFix by overriding the parent's `point(inside:with:)` to also return true for the button's frame, or override `hitTest` to return the button. This is exactly how you enlarge a small button's tap target, which is the most-asked version of this question." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you increase a button\'s tap target without changing its visual size?',
      a: "```\noverride func point(inside p: CGPoint, with e: UIEvent?) -> Bool {\n    bounds.insetBy(dx: -10, dy: -10).contains(p)\n}\n```\n\nA negative inset grows the hit rectangle. The alternative in modern UIKit is `UIButton.Configuration` with `contentInsets`, which changes the actual frame. Apple's HIG minimum is 44x44 points, and this override is how you honour that without a 44pt icon." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the first responder, and how do you manage it?',
      a: "The object at the head of the responder chain, typically the focused text input. `becomeFirstResponder()` requests it, `resignFirstResponder()` gives it up, and `canBecomeFirstResponder` gates whether a responder is eligible.\n\n`view.endEditing(true)` walks the hierarchy and resigns whatever holds it, which is the reliable way to dismiss a keyboard when you do not have a reference to the specific field." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'How do gesture recognizers interact with touch delivery to views?',
      a: "Gesture recognizers get the touches **before** the hit-tested view. While a recognizer is in `.possible`, touches go to both. When a recognizer **recognises**, it by default sends `touchesCancelled` to the view and takes over, which is why a `UITapGestureRecognizer` on a superview can silently swallow a button's touch.\n\nControls: `cancelsTouchesInView` (set false to let the view keep them), `delaysTouchesBegan`, `delaysTouchesEnded`, and the delegate methods `shouldRecognizeSimultaneouslyWith`, `shouldRequireFailureOf` and `shouldReceive touch`." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'Two gesture recognizers conflict. What is your toolkit?',
      a: "- `require(toFail:)`: the classic single-tap versus double-tap resolution, and it costs a delay on the single tap.\n- `UIGestureRecognizerDelegate.shouldRecognizeSimultaneouslyWith`: let both fire, for example pinch plus rotate.\n- `shouldBeRequiredToFail` / `shouldRequireFailureOf`: dynamic precedence.\n- `gestureRecognizer(_:shouldReceive:)`: reject touches in a region entirely, which is the cleanest fix for 'my tap-to-dismiss also fires on the sheet content'.\n\nIn a scroll view, `panGestureRecognizer` participates too, which is why a custom pan inside a scroll view usually needs simultaneous recognition." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What is the difference between `touchesBegan` and a UITapGestureRecognizer?',
      a: "`touchesBegan/Moved/Ended/Cancelled` is the raw event API on `UIResponder`: you get every touch and must build state machines yourself. A gesture recognizer is a reusable, prebuilt state machine that coordinates with other recognizers through the failure and simultaneity system.\n\nUse recognizers by default. Drop to raw touches only for genuinely custom multi-touch interaction, and remember you must handle `touchesCancelled`, which is the step people forget." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'Why might `isUserInteractionEnabled` on a parent break an unrelated child?',
      a: "It disables the whole subtree for hit testing. `hitTest` returns nil immediately for a view with interaction disabled, and never examines its subviews, so every descendant becomes untouchable regardless of its own setting.\n\nThe same total-blackout effect comes from `alpha <= 0.01` on an ancestor. That is a real trap when you fade a container to 0 to hide it rather than setting `isHidden`, since a 0-alpha container also blocks nothing... it blocks everything beneath it only if it still receives touches, which it does not, so touches fall through to whatever is behind. Either way the behaviour surprises people." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What does `UIControl` add over a plain view with a tap recognizer?',
      a: "The target-action mechanism with a rich set of `UIControl.Event` values: `touchDown`, `touchUpInside`, `touchDragExit`, `valueChanged`, and so on. It tracks the touch through drag, so `touchUpInside` correctly does **not** fire if the finger leaves the bounds before lifting.\n\nA tap recognizer does not give you that. Reimplementing drag-out cancellation, highlight states and accessibility traits on a plain view is why `UIControl` exists." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { d: 'medium', q: 'hitTest recurses through subviews in:', choices: ['Subview array order, front to back', 'Reverse subview order, topmost first', 'Alphabetical order by tag', 'Order of constraint installation'], correct: 1,
      why: 'The last subview is drawn on top and must win the touch, so hit testing walks in reverse.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'A button positioned outside its parent bounds is untappable. The fix is:', choices: ['Set clipsToBounds = false', 'Override point(inside:with:) or hitTest on the parent', 'Raise the button zPosition', 'Set isUserInteractionEnabled on the button'], correct: 1,
      why: 'clipsToBounds affects drawing only. Hit testing stops at the parent because point(inside:) returns false.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'A view is skipped by hit testing when:', choices: ['alpha <= 0.01, isHidden, or isUserInteractionEnabled is false', 'It has no subviews', 'It has no gesture recognizers', 'Its backgroundColor is clear'], correct: 0,
      why: 'A clear background is still hit-testable, which is why invisible-but-present views intercept taps unexpectedly.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'A tap recognizer on a superview stops a button working. The most targeted fix:', choices: ['Remove the recognizer', 'Set cancelsTouchesInView = false, or reject the touch in gestureRecognizer(_:shouldReceive:)', 'Set delaysTouchesBegan = true', 'Move the button above the recognizer view'], correct: 1,
      why: 'On recognition the recognizer cancels touches in the view by default. Either stop cancelling, or refuse touches landing on controls.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'touchUpInside does NOT fire if:', choices: ['The touch lasted over a second', 'The finger left the control bounds before lifting', 'The control is inside a scroll view', 'Two fingers were used'], correct: 1,
      why: 'UIControl tracks the touch through drag. That drag-out cancellation is a key reason to use UIControl over a tap recognizer.' },
    { d: 'easy', q: 'The minimum recommended tap target per the HIG is:', choices: ['24x24 pt', '32x32 pt', '44x44 pt', '60x60 pt'], correct: 2,
      why: 'Enlarge via point(inside:) with a negative inset when the visual element is smaller than 44pt.' }
  ]
});

IPREP.addTopic({
  id: 'coreanimation', domain: 'uikit',
  title: 'Core Animation & CALayer',
  summary: 'The layer tree, implicit vs explicit animation, and the model/presentation split.',
  cards: [
    { d: 'easy', q: 'What are the three layer trees?',
      a: "- **Model tree**: the `CALayer` objects you set properties on. Reading a property gives you the final value, even mid-animation.\n- **Presentation tree**: what is on screen right now. `layer.presentation()` gives you the interpolated in-flight values.\n- **Render tree**: private to the render server, used for compositing.\n\nThis split is why hit testing a moving view fails: the model layer is already at the destination while the pixels are still travelling." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What is implicit animation and why does it not happen on UIView layers?',
      a: "Setting an animatable `CALayer` property normally creates an implicit animation with a default 0.25s duration, driven by the layer's action lookup. But a layer that backs a `UIView` returns `NSNull` from `action(for:)` outside an animation block, which disables implicit animation.\n\nSo `view.layer.opacity = 0` snaps, while a standalone `CALayer` you added yourself fades. That asymmetry surprises people who add a sublayer and find it animating its frame on every `layoutSubviews`." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'How do you stop a standalone sublayer from animating its frame on every layout?',
      a: "Wrap the change in a transaction with actions disabled:\n\n```\nCATransaction.begin()\nCATransaction.setDisableActions(true)\ngradientLayer.frame = bounds\nCATransaction.commit()\n```\n\nOr return `NSNull` from the layer delegate's `action(for:forKey:)`. Without this, resizing on rotation or keyboard appearance produces a visible quarter-second lag as the sublayer animates to catch up." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Explain `fillMode` and `isRemovedOnCompletion`.',
      a: "A `CAAnimation` does not change the model layer; it only affects the presentation. When it finishes it is removed and the layer snaps back to its model value.\n\nSetting `isRemovedOnCompletion = false` plus `fillMode = .forwards` makes it appear to hold the end state. That is a **lie**: the model value is unchanged, so hit testing, subsequent layout and any read of the property all use the old value. The correct fix is to set the model property to the final value and animate from the old one." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'What is the difference between UIView animation and CAAnimation?',
      a: "`UIView.animate` sets the model property **and** creates the animation, so the end state is real. `CABasicAnimation` only creates the animation; you must set the model value yourself.\n\nUIView animations are also implemented on top of Core Animation and are limited to animatable `UIView` properties. Drop to `CAAnimation` when you need keyframes on a path, custom timing functions per keyframe, `CAAnimationGroup`, layer-only properties like `shadowPath` or a `CAShapeLayer`'s `strokeEnd`, or fine control over `beginTime` for choreography." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What is `anchorPoint` and how does it interact with `position`?',
      a: "`anchorPoint` is a unit-space point, default `(0.5, 0.5)`, that defines both the origin of transforms and the point that `position` places. Setting `anchorPoint` to `(0, 0)` makes rotation pivot around the top-left **and** moves the layer, because `position` now refers to the top-left.\n\nTo change the anchor without moving the layer, adjust `position` by the delta. This is the whole trick behind rotating a hand around a clock's centre." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'What does `shouldRasterize` do and when is it a win?',
      a: "It renders the layer and its sublayers once into a bitmap and reuses that bitmap on subsequent frames, at `rasterizationScale`. It is a win when a **complex, static** subtree is composited repeatedly, for example a detailed card being translated across the screen.\n\nIt is a loss when the content changes, because every change invalidates the cache and forces a re-render, which is strictly worse than not caching. Forgetting `rasterizationScale = UIScreen.main.scale` produces blurry output on Retina. Verify with Color Hits Green and Misses Red in the simulator." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Which CALayer properties are animatable, and which are the useful specialised layers?',
      a: "Animatable: `position`, `bounds`, `opacity`, `transform`, `backgroundColor`, `cornerRadius`, `borderWidth`, `shadowOpacity`, `shadowPath`, and for `CAShapeLayer`, `path` and `strokeEnd`.\n\nSpecialised layers worth knowing by name: `CAShapeLayer` (vector paths, animatable stroke), `CAGradientLayer`, `CAEmitterLayer` (particles), `CAReplicatorLayer`, `CATextLayer`, `AVPlayerLayer`, `CATransformLayer` (true 3D). Naming three or four of these in an interview signals real Core Animation experience." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'How do you animate drawing a path, like a checkmark?',
      a: "A `CAShapeLayer` with the path set, then animate `strokeEnd` from 0 to 1:\n\n```\nlet a = CABasicAnimation(keyPath: \"strokeEnd\")\na.fromValue = 0; a.toValue = 1\na.duration = 0.4\na.timingFunction = CAMediaTimingFunction(name: .easeOut)\nlayer.strokeEnd = 1\nlayer.add(a, forKey: \"draw\")\n```\n\nNote the model value is set before adding the animation, so the layer holds the final state without `fillMode` tricks. This is the canonical Core Animation interview answer." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Why does a view being animated not respond to taps where you see it?',
      a: "Hit testing uses the **model** layer, which already holds the destination value, while your eye sees the presentation layer mid-flight. So the touchable region is at the end position, not the visible one.\n\nIf you need the visible region to be tappable during an animation, hit test against `layer.presentation()`, or use `UIViewPropertyAnimator`, whose interactive animations keep the model in step, or set `.allowUserInteraction` on the UIView animation options." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Reading `layer.opacity` during an animation gives you:', choices: ['The current on-screen value', 'The final model value', 'Zero', 'The initial value'], correct: 1,
      why: 'The model tree holds the destination. Use layer.presentation() for the in-flight interpolated value.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'isRemovedOnCompletion = false with fillMode = .forwards is discouraged because:', choices: ['It leaks memory', 'The model value is unchanged, so layout and hit testing use the old value', 'It disables the GPU', 'It only works on iOS 12'], correct: 1,
      why: 'It makes the layer look finished while the real state is stale. Set the model property and animate from the old value instead.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'A CAGradientLayer you added animates its frame on every rotation. Fix:', choices: ['Set it as the view backing layer', 'Wrap the frame change in a CATransaction with setDisableActions(true)', 'Use UIView.animate with duration 0', 'Set masksToBounds'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Standalone layers get implicit animations. View-backed layers return NSNull from action(for:) and do not.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'shouldRasterize hurts performance when:', choices: ['The subtree is static and complex', 'The content changes frequently, invalidating the cache every frame', 'The layer is small', 'rasterizationScale is set correctly'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Every invalidation forces a re-render plus the cache write, which is strictly worse than compositing directly.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'To animate a checkmark drawing itself you animate:', choices: ['CAShapeLayer.strokeEnd', 'CALayer.opacity', 'CAGradientLayer.locations', 'CALayer.mask'], correct: 0,
      why: 'strokeEnd controls how much of the path is stroked, from 0 to 1, which reads as drawing.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'Changing anchorPoint to (0,0) also moves the layer because:', choices: ['bounds is recalculated', 'position refers to the anchor point, which has moved', 'The transform is reset', 'The superlayer relaid out'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Compensate by adjusting position by the delta if you want to change the pivot without moving the layer.' }
  ]
});

IPREP.addTopic({
  id: 'navigation', domain: 'uikit',
  title: 'Navigation, Coordinators & Deep Linking',
  summary: 'Who decides what screen comes next, and how to keep view controllers reusable.',
  cards: [
    { d: 'easy', q: 'What problem does the coordinator pattern solve?',
      a: "In plain UIKit, a view controller pushes the next view controller, which means it knows about its successor, constructs its dependencies, and cannot be reused in another flow. Navigation logic is scattered across every screen.\n\nA **coordinator** owns the flow: it creates view controllers, injects dependencies, and decides what happens on each outcome. The view controller just reports events, usually through a delegate or a closure. Screens become reusable and the flow becomes testable and readable in one file." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Sketch the shape of a coordinator.',
      a: "```\nprotocol Coordinator: AnyObject {\n    var childCoordinators: [Coordinator] { get set }\n    func start()\n}\n\nfinal class OnboardingCoordinator: Coordinator {\n    var childCoordinators: [Coordinator] = []\n    private let nav: UINavigationController\n    init(nav: UINavigationController) { self.nav = nav }\n    func start() {\n        let vc = WelcomeViewController()\n        vc.onContinue = { [weak self] in self?.showSignUp() }\n        nav.pushViewController(vc, animated: false)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe parent holds children in `childCoordinators`, and the crucial discipline is **removing** a child when its flow ends, or you leak the whole subtree." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'What is the hardest part of coordinators in practice?',
      a: "Child lifecycle. Nothing tells the parent that a flow ended when the user swipes back or dismisses a sheet, so children accumulate and leak.\n\nSolutions: have the child call a `didFinish(self)` on a parent delegate; implement `UINavigationControllerDelegate.didShow` and compare the stack against your children; or use `UIAdaptivePresentationControllerDelegate.presentationControllerDidDismiss` for sheets. Every real coordinator implementation has some version of this bookkeeping, and if a candidate does not mention it they have not shipped one." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you pass data back from a pushed view controller?',
      a: "In order of preference:\n1. A **closure** on the pushed controller, set by the coordinator. Simple, one-shot, no protocol boilerplate.\n2. A **delegate** protocol, when there are several related callbacks.\n3. A shared observable model or a Combine/async stream, when the data outlives the screen.\n\nWhat you should not do is reach up the navigation stack, `navigationController?.viewControllers[0] as? HomeVC`, which couples the screens and crashes when the stack changes." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'How would you implement deep linking cleanly?',
      a: "Parse the URL into a typed `Route` enum, then hand it to a router that walks or rebuilds the coordinator tree:\n\n```\nenum Route { case product(id: String), settings, checkout(cart: String) }\n```\n\nKey decisions to state out loud:\n- **Rebuild versus navigate**: from a cold launch you construct the whole stack; from a warm state you may need to dismiss modals first.\n- **Authentication**: a route may require login, so the router must be able to queue the route and replay it after auth.\n- **Validation**: an unknown or malformed URL must fall back gracefully, never crash.\n\nParsing into a typed route first is what makes all three tractable." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'What is `UINavigationController` actually managing?',
      a: "A stack of view controllers, plus the navigation bar and the interactive pop gesture. `pushViewController`, `popViewController`, `popToRootViewController` and `setViewControllers(_:animated:)` mutate the stack.\n\n`setViewControllers` is the one people forget: it replaces the whole stack with an animation, which is how you implement 'complete this flow and land on the detail screen with a sensible back button' without pushing three screens the user never sees." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'What breaks when you present a modal from a view controller that is not visible?',
      a: "You get 'Attempt to present X on Y whose view is not in the window hierarchy'. It happens when you present from `viewDidLoad`, or from a controller that has already been dismissed or is itself covered by another modal.\n\nThe fix is to present from the **topmost presented controller**, and to present no earlier than `viewDidAppear`. A coordinator helps because it holds a reference to the presenting context rather than assuming `self` is on screen." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'Storyboard segues versus programmatic navigation, what is the real trade-off?',
      a: "Segues give you a visual map of the flow and less boilerplate for simple apps. The costs are real: merge conflicts in a large XML file, `prepare(for:sender:)` with stringly-typed identifiers and casts, dependency injection that is awkward at best, and no way to unit test a flow.\n\nMost teams past a certain size move to programmatic navigation, often keeping storyboards or XIBs for individual screen layout while doing all navigation in code." },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'How do you unit test navigation logic?',
      a: "Make the coordinator's decisions observable and its dependencies injectable. Give it a navigation abstraction rather than a concrete `UINavigationController`:\n\n```\nprotocol Navigator { func push(_ vc: UIViewController, animated: Bool) }\n```\n\nThen the test drives the coordinator with a spy navigator and asserts on the type of controller pushed and the order. The point is that the flow logic is now in a plain object with no view lifecycle, so it tests in milliseconds without a host application." },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'How do you handle navigation state restoration?',
      a: "Encode the current route stack, not the view controllers. `NSUserActivity` is the system-supported mechanism and it also feeds Handoff, Spotlight and Siri suggestions. Persist a lightweight array of your `Route` enum, and on launch replay it through the same router used for deep links.\n\nThat reuse is the whole argument for typed routes: restoration, deep links, push notification taps and widget taps all become the same code path." }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { d: 'medium', q: 'The main benefit of the coordinator pattern is:', choices: ['Faster rendering', 'View controllers no longer know about their successors, so they become reusable and the flow is testable', 'Less memory usage', 'It removes the need for a navigation controller'], correct: 1,
      why: 'It moves flow decisions out of the screens, which is what makes both reuse and unit testing possible.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'The most common coordinator bug is:', choices: ['Retain cycles between coordinator and view controller', 'Failing to remove child coordinators when a flow ends, leaking the subtree', 'Pushing on the wrong thread', 'Not using storyboards'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Nothing signals flow completion on a back swipe or sheet dismissal, so you need explicit didFinish bookkeeping.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: '"Attempt to present on a view controller whose view is not in the window hierarchy" usually means:', choices: ['You presented too early, or from a controller no longer on screen', 'The modal has no storyboard', 'You forgot animated: true', 'The view controller has no navigation controller'], correct: 0,
      why: 'Present from the topmost presented controller, and not before viewDidAppear.' },
    { d: 'hard', q: 'Parsing a deep link into a typed Route enum first is valuable mainly because:', choices: ['It is faster than string comparison', 'Deep links, restoration, push taps and widget taps then share one code path', 'It avoids URL encoding bugs', 'It is required by iOS'], correct: 1,
      why: 'One typed route plus one router handles every entry point, including the auth-required and cold-launch cases.' },
    { d: 'medium', q: 'To replace the whole navigation stack with an animation you use:', choices: ['popToRootViewController', 'setViewControllers(_:animated:)', 'pushViewController repeatedly', 'dismiss(animated:)'], correct: 1,
      why: 'It lands the user on the right screen with a sensible back stack, without animating through screens they never asked for.' },
    { d: 'easy', q: 'Passing data back from a pushed controller is best done with:', choices: ['Reaching into navigationController.viewControllers and casting', 'A closure or delegate the parent or coordinator sets', 'A global singleton', 'NotificationCenter'], correct: 1,
      why: 'Reaching up the stack couples the screens and breaks the moment the stack composition changes.' }
  ]
});
